<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Bundi State</h1><p class="st-meta">1342 CE – 1948 CE · capital Bundi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Hada Chauhan Rajput kingdom of Hadoti in south-eastern Rajasthan, known for the Taragarh fort, its step-wells and the Bundi school of painting.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1342 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-deva">Rao Deva</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hada-chauhans">Hada</a> branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhans</a>, took the Bundi area from local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena-people">Meena</a> chiefs and made it his capital. The surrounding region became known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadoti">Hadoti</a> after his clan.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bundi's Raos often fought <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/udaipur-state">Mewar</a> and later served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-surjan">Rao Surjan</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranthambore-fort">Ranthambore</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> in 1569, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-chhatrasal-of-bundi">Rao Chhatrasal</a> was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-samugarh">Samugarh</a> in 1658 fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dara-shikoh">Dara Shikoh</a>. The neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kotah-state">Kotah State</a> split off in 1631. The court sponsored a distinctive school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-miniature">miniature painting</a>, and Bundi is known for its step-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taragarh-fort-bundi">Taragarh</a> fort.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bundi suffered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> raids and signed a treaty with the British in 1818. It joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan</a> in 1948 and is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/bundi-state.jpg" alt="Bundi State" width="320" height="204"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bundi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1342 CE – 1948 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bundi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Rao Deva, Rao Surjan, Rao Chhatrasal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hadoti</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bundi</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1645&amp;focus=bundi-state" aria-label="Bundi State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6683 1242 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Bundi State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7003 1498L7018 1385M7003 1498L7364 1601M7003 1498L7364 1602M7003 1498L6996 1446M7003 1498L7010 1507"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6996 1446h0M7010 1507h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7003 1498h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7003 1498h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bundi. <a href="/atlas?year=1645&amp;focus=bundi-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
